---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import BaseLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import HomeOpening from "../components/HomeOpening.astro";
import "../styles/home.css";
import HomeCorridor from "../components/HomeCorridor.astro";
import SemesterTimeline from "../components/SemesterTimeline.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { tutorialMarking } from "../lib/assessment-plan";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  mainAttrs={{ class: "home-content" }}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <HomeOpening slot="hero" />

  <section id="hill" aria-labelledby="home-corridor-title">
    <h2 id="home-corridor-title">One decision to try now</h2>
    <HomeCorridor />
  </section>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Design and improve the algorithms, one constraint at a time. The problem grows in five
    steps: find a route (weeks 1–3); find a route the drone can actually fly and return from
    (week 4); order many deliveries for one drone (weeks 5–6); divide the work between drones
    that share two charging pads (weeks 7–8); make the plans of five drones executable
    together in one shared airspace (weeks 9–12). Each step starts from the method you
    already have, breaks it on a small example, and makes you change the state, the cost or
    the check that was wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every week you write a prediction before you run anything, derive one thing by hand,
    implement one key function, test it against a counterexample, and explain the result
    under the objective the course fixed. You leave with a cumulative planner — search,
    scheduling, space-time coordination — and the arguments, tests and records that say what
    it guarantees and what it does not.
  </p>

  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <section class="learning-outcomes" aria-labelledby="learning-outcomes-title">
    <h3 id="learning-outcomes-title">Learning outcomes</h3>
    <p>By the end of this course, you will be able to:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Model a delivery task as a weighted graph, implement Dijkstra and A*, and explain when their results are optimal.</li>
      <li>Check a complete delivery against payload, energy and reserve limits, including service and the flight home.</li>
      <li>Build and improve delivery schedules that account for ready times, deadlines, different drones and shared charging pads.</li>
      <li>Coordinate flights using timed reservations and space-time search, then update the schedule when route costs change.</li>
      <li>Compare algorithms under the same conditions, reproduce an experiment, and explain failures and the limits of your evidence.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year undergraduates who have done introductory programming and data structures:
    functions, loops, lists and dictionaries, queues and priority queues, basic graphs, and
    enough algebra to sum costs along a path. No robotics, no flight control, no prior
    optimisation course. If you can write a loop that pops the smallest item from a queue,
    you can start; if you want a course about real drones and real airspace rules, this is
    not it — every number here is a teaching setting, and the policies page says so.
  </p>

  <SemesterTimeline
    title="How it is assessed"
    introduction={`${courseMeta.code} runs in ${courseMeta.session}, ${courseMeta.year}, over ${tutorialMarking.weeks} teaching weeks.`}
  >
    <div slot="overview" class="course-rhythm">
      <p>
        Each week pairs a <strong>one-hour Monday lecture</strong> with a <strong>two-hour Wednesday tutorial</strong>.
        The lecture develops the model and
        algorithm; the tutorial asks you to predict, try a small case and explain what
        happened. Finish your implementation, tests and reports outside class.
      </p>
      <p>
        In weeks 1–6, you build the search and single-drone schedule used in <strong>Assignment 1</strong>.
        In weeks 7–12, you extend that work to fleet
        assignment, shared resources and coordinated flights for <strong>Assignment 2</strong>.
        Both assignments are individual and assess your code, validation and explanation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Every tutorial counts towards your grade.</strong> All {tutorialMarking.weeks} sessions
        contribute equally to the tutorial component through attendance and lab
        participation. The timeline brings those weekly sessions together with the
        assignment weights, release dates and deadlines. Select a lecture, tutorial or
        assignment to open its page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SemesterTimeline>

  <section class="home-next" aria-labelledby="home-next-title">
  <h2 id="home-next-title">Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href={withBase("/lectures/")}>
      <p>Twelve Monday lectures, one question each, from a graph to a plan that flies.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href={withBase("/sessions/")}>
      <p>Twelve Wednesday tutorials: predict, derive, implement, test, explain.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href={withBase("/assessments/")}>
      <p>Tutorial participation, the two assignments, their dates, weights and marking criteria.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lab" href={withBase("/lab/")}>
      <p>Every week's workbench on one page, opened in order by the semester slider.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
